{
  "statement": "Strict liability calibrated to risk tier — not firm size — is the enforcement backbone without which every other AI safety commitment is performative. Voluntary safety practices collapse under competitive pressure. The documented pattern is consistent: when OpenAI compressed GPT-4's red-teaming window, when Microsoft dissolved its AI ethics team in March 2023, when Anthropic and Google raced deployment timelines in 2024, safety investment moved in one direction. Liability is the mechanism that reverses that gradient by making safety engineering economically rational before deployment, not after harm. The precedent is not speculative. Auto manufacturers built crumple zones because tort verdicts made them cheaper than lawsuits. Pharmaceutical companies run Phase III trials because the alternative is ruinous. Aviation achieved its safety record because Boeing and Airbus internalize crash costs through liability and insurance.\n\nThe strongest accelerationist objection deserves a direct answer: a poorly designed liability regime could concentrate the market among well-capitalized incumbents and chill experimentation by smaller developers. That concern is real for low-stakes applications. It collapses for high-risk autonomous systems deployed in critical infrastructure, medical triage, or lethal autonomy. A two-person startup deploying an autonomous decision system into a hospital ICU is precisely the actor least equipped to absorb the safety engineering costs that deployment context demands. The equity question is not whether small developers face barriers — it is who bears the cost when an under-resourced system fails irreversibly. Risk-tier calibration, anchored to irreversibility and deployment criticality, resolves this without creating liability shields for catastrophic harm categories.\n\nSafe harbors and the state-of-the-art defense function as liability sinks. They let developers externalize harms onto deployers, fine-tuners, and end users through diffuse causation chains that no plaintiff can untangle. The EU AI Act's Article 6 high-risk categories and the revised Product Liability Directive both moved to narrow these defenses for autonomous systems — Congress should follow. Joint-and-several liability across the AI supply chain, with developers bearing primary responsibility for foreseeable misuse, forces upstream safety-gated deployment instead of passing risk downstream. Strict liability is designed precisely to cut through causation ambiguity by placing the burden on the party that controls system design. That is its feature, not its bug.\n\nThis is not anti-market. It is the market mechanism that makes safety a competitive advantage rather than a deadweight cost. Competition drives safety only when failure carries real consequences. Otherwise it drives the opposite — and we have four years of frontier lab behavior to confirm that. Reframe the debate honestly: the choice is not liability versus innovation, but internalizing externalities versus socializing catastrophic risk onto third parties who never consented to bear it. Accelerationists who oppose liability should be required to name the specific concrete harms — the hospital deaths, the infrastructure failures, the discriminatory denials — they consider acceptable losses for unencumbered deployment.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this argument. First, that ex-ante engineering choices meaningfully determine ex-post harm rates — that safety is engineerable, not merely probabilistic. If that is wrong, liability still allocates loss but stops driving design improvements, and the case shifts toward insurance pooling and mandatory pre-deployment testing. Second, that courts can adjudicate AI harm causation under a strict liability standard. If they cannot, the regime needs statutory presumptions and burden-shifting rules of the kind already used in toxic tort and defective product cases — not abandonment of the underlying principle.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚓", "tooltip": "Strict liability is like an anchor; it holds safety commitments in place when competitive currents would otherwise drag them out to sea. But if developers can cut the line through safe harbors and state-of-the-art defenses, what exactly is anchoring anything?"},
    {"symbol": "🏗️", "tooltip": "Risk-tier calibration is like building codes scaled to occupancy; a backyard shed and a hospital tower face different load requirements. So why should a chatbot and an autonomous ICU triage system face the same liability standard?"}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Strict liability calibrated to risk tier — not firm size — is the enforcement backbone without which every other AI safety commitment is performative.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Voluntary safety practices collapse under competitive pressure, as the dissolution of Microsoft's AI ethics team and compressed red-teaming windows at frontier labs demonstrate.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "A two-person startup deploying an autonomous decision system into a hospital ICU is precisely the actor least equipped to absorb the safety engineering costs that deployment context demands.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Safe harbors and the state-of-the-art defense function as liability sinks that let developers externalize harms onto deployers and end users through diffuse causation chains.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Competition drives safety only when failure carries real consequences; otherwise it drives the opposite.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "Accelerationists who oppose liability should be required to name the specific concrete harms they consider acceptable losses for unencumbered deployment.", "targets": []}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Ex-ante engineering choices meaningfully determine ex-post AI harm rates — safety is engineerable, not purely probabilistic.", "if_wrong": "Liability still allocates loss fairly but stops driving design improvements; the policy emphasis would shift toward mandatory insurance pooling and pre-deployment testing regimes."},
    {"assumption": "Courts can adjudicate AI harm causation under a strict liability standard with appropriate statutory presumptions.", "if_wrong": "The regime would need stronger burden-shifting rules and administrative adjudication bodies modeled on vaccine injury compensation, rather than relying primarily on tort litigation."}
  ]
}

