  "statement": "Safetyist makes the strongest case in the room: voluntary safety collapses under competitive pressure, and ex-post liability is the enforcement backbone that pre-deployment audits cannot replace. The auto, pharma, and aviation analogies are not foolish — those regimes did discipline reckless engineering. But they worked because crash forensics, pharmacokinetics, and flight data recorders gave plaintiffs and regulators a causation science. AI's black-box opacity has no such infrastructure yet, and that absence is the load-bearing fact this debate keeps stepping around.\n\nStrict liability without discovery rights is theater. Plaintiffs in algorithmic discrimination cases — hiring at HireVue, lending under ECOA challenges, COMPAS-style criminal risk scoring — already cannot obtain training data, model weights, or decision traces. Existing negligence suits stall at causation, and a strict liability label does not magically produce the audit trail courts need to assign fault. Toxic-tort burden-shifting, which Safetyist gestured at, required decades of statutory architecture — Daubert standards, mandatory disclosure regimes, agency epidemiology. None of that scaffolding exists for foundation models. Until Congress mandates cryptographically verifiable audit trails and statutory burden-shifting onto developers, debating strict-versus-negligence is debating the color of a door no plaintiff can reach.\n\nThe horizontal 'high-risk autonomous system' category is also the wrong instrument. It lumps ICU triage with resume screening and invites exactly the capture both prior speakers should worry about. The EU AI Act's high-risk tier has already produced compliance-cost asymmetries that OpenAI and Anthropic pre-pay as a line item while open-source developers cannot absorb them. Existing domain frameworks — FDA 510(k) for medical devices, FAA Part 25 for avionics, ECOA and HMDA for lending — already encode context-specific causation rules and small-actor calibration. Sector-specific liability outperforms a novel horizontal regime that incumbents will lobby to shape into a moat. Safetyist's two-person ICU startup hypothetical proves my point, not theirs: that case is solved by FDA pathways, not by a unified 'autonomous decision-making' category.\n\nThe actual obstruction to accountability is not strict liability itself but the machinery surrounding it — the state-of-the-art defense, supply-chain diffusion, and jurisdictional arbitrage. Foundation-model providers headquarter in favorable jurisdictions, disclaim downstream use in their terms of service, and push liability onto deployers through indemnification clauses. Limited-scope third-party audits launder those defenses; this is the audit-washing problem documented in algorithmic accountability research from Raji, Costanza-Chock, and others. The harms being socialized today are not extinction scenarios — they are wage suppression through algorithmic management, discriminatory denial in credit and housing, and financial misrepresentation by generative systems. Accelerationist's 'cost of inaction' framing ignores that ledger entirely.\n\nThe operational answer couples pre-deployment audits with post-deployment liability, anchored to documented harms and calibrated by domain. That means mandatory architectural verification — model cards with teeth, logged inference traces, third-party access rights modeled on SEC examination authority — paired with sector-specific liability that imports product liability's risk-utility test for defective design. Small-developer exemptions apply to low-stakes deployment, not to medical triage or pretrial risk scoring. Innovation by whom, safety for whom: those are the questions a reporter should be asking when the next hearing convenes, not whether liability is on or off.",
