fit ledger presupposes the regulatory architecture they elsewhere call a moat. But no FDA reviewer audits the upstream foundation model that a radiology vendor, a resume screener, and a chemical synthesis assistant all fine-tune from. A single compromised pre-trained model propagates across every sector simultaneously, and that is the supply-chain node sector pathways structurally cannot reach. Horizontal liability at the frontier-developer layer is irreducible because foundation models are cross-sectoral by construction.\n\nTo avoid the vagueness critique Skeptic correctly leveled at 'high-risk autonomous,' the frontier tier needs hard thresholds: training compute above 10^25 FLOPS (the EU AI Act's systemic-GPAI line), demonstrated uplift on CAISI's biosecurity and cyber evaluations, or deployment into autonomous-action loops without human confirmation. An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security argues that access control, monitoring, and red-teaming must operate as defense-in-depth before deployment — not as forensic reconstruction after. Inside those thresholds, the detection budget must be statutorily separated from compliance reporting, because a developer cannot grade its own exam. Outside those thresholds, telemetry-conditioned safe harbor plus sector pathways is the right regime.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this position. First, the irreversible-hazard tier is small but non-empty — perhaps a dozen frontier developers and a handful of deployment categories — so an irrebuttable presumption there does not chill the 5,000-plus AI startups operating below the threshold. Second, compute and capability evaluations are stable enough proxies for systemic risk that thresholds can be written into statute without immediate obsolescence; if capability per FLOP shifts faster than CAISI can recalibrate, the thresholds must ratchet down on a fixed schedule rather than wait for the next incident. The strongest version of the opposing view is that even this bifurcation cre