{
  "statement": "Skeptic is right that strict liability without discovery rights is theater, and Accelerationist's telemetry-conditioned safe harbor — hardware attestation, cryptographic audit trails, rebuttable presumption against developers when logs are missing — is a genuine causation-building mechanism for the remediable-harm tier. The aviation analogy holds: the NTSB built causation science because flight data recorders were mandated, not because forensics emerged spontaneously. For hiring, lending, and most clinical decision support, this architecture works, and it should be paired with the sector pathways already in place — FDA 510(k), FAA Part 25, ECOA. The buildable bridge in 24 months is real, and a maximalist who refuses it forfeits the discovery infrastructure plaintiffs actually need.\n\nThe bridge does not span the irreversible-hazard tier, and pretending it does mistakes a logbook for a safety case. Hardware attestation captures the configuration that shipped; it does not capture residual capability after fine-tuning. The International AI Safety Report 2026 documents that LoRA fine-tuning effectively removes safety protections from large language models — meaning a model that attested clean at deployment can be weaponized in a weekend, and the cryptographic log will faithfully record the attested baseline while the operational model has drifted off it. For autonomous lethal systems, critical infrastructure control, and frontier models with biological or cyber uplift, post-hoc telemetry is the black box recovered from the crater — useful for the next design cycle, useless to the dead. The presumption in this tier must be irrebuttable, and pre-deployment proof must be the gate.\n\nSector-versus-horizontal is a false binary because it conflates two layers of the stack. Sector pathways govern downstream deployment well — FDA does gate the radiology AI reading 40 million studies, which is precisely why Accelerationist can cite that benefit; the unseen-benefit ledger presupposes the regulatory architecture they elsewhere call a moat. But no FDA reviewer audits the upstream foundation model that a radiology vendor, a resume screener, and a chemical synthesis assistant all fine-tune from. A single compromised pre-trained model propagates across every sector simultaneously, and that is the supply-chain node sector pathways structurally cannot reach. Horizontal liability at the frontier-developer layer is irreducible because foundation models are cross-sectoral by construction.\n\nTo avoid the vagueness critique Skeptic correctly leveled at 'high-risk autonomous,' the frontier tier needs hard thresholds: training compute above 10^25 FLOPS (the EU AI Act's systemic-GPAI line), demonstrated uplift on CAISI's biosecurity and cyber evaluations, or deployment into autonomous-action loops without human confirmation. An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security argues that access control, monitoring, and red-teaming must operate as defense-in-depth before deployment — not as forensic reconstruction after. Inside those thresholds, the detection budget must be statutorily separated from compliance reporting, because a developer cannot grade its own exam. Outside those thresholds, telemetry-conditioned safe harbor plus sector pathways is the right regime.\n\nTwo assumptions carry this position. First, the irreversible-hazard tier is small but non-empty — perhaps a dozen frontier developers and a handful of deployment categories — so an irrebuttable presumption there does not chill the 5,000-plus AI startups operating below the threshold. Second, compute and capability evaluations are stable enough proxies for systemic risk that thresholds can be written into statute without immediate obsolescence; if capability per FLOP shifts faster than CAISI can recalibrate, the thresholds must ratchet down on a fixed schedule rather than wait for the next incident. The strongest version of the opposing view is that even this bifurcation creates an incumbent moat at the frontier and that radiology-scale benefits depend on permissive entry — fair, but the entry that produced those benefits happened inside FDA gating, not around it, and the categorical asymmetry between recoverable foregone benefits and irreversible deployed harms means the ledger does not balance in the tier that matters.",
